Q: Modifying a class list results in no modification of the list A: <p>Both of your proposed list updating methods work (although the list comprehension version creates a new list instead of modifying the existing list object). The real problem is that you did not recalculate <code>self.NumofPossibilities</code> after updating <code>self.possibilities</code>:</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>    def eliminate(self, letter) -&gt; None:
        if re.search(r&quot;\b[A-Z]\b&quot;, letter, re.IGNORECASE) and type(letter) == str:
            self.possibilities = [word for word in self.possibilities if not letter.upper() in word]
            self.NumofPossibilities = len(self.possibilities)  # add this
        else:
            raise TypeError(&quot;'letter' argument can only be one of the 26 letters of the alphabet.&quot;)
</code></pre>
<hr />
<p>A better solution is to use the <a href="https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#property" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>@property</code></a> decorator, which makes it recalculate the length on the fly:</p>
<pre class="lang-py prettyprint-override"><code>class Word:
    possibilities = [&quot;APPLE&quot;, &quot;MANGO&quot;, &quot;YOYLE&quot;, &quot;BERRY&quot;, &quot;DREAM&quot;, &quot;ISLES&quot;, &quot;PHONE&quot;, &quot;MEPAD&quot;, &quot;POMNI&quot;, &quot;STEVE&quot;, &quot;CAINE&quot;]

    def __init__(self):
        self.possibilities = Word.possibilities
        # remove this: self.NumofPossibilities = len(self.possibilities)

    def eliminate(self, letter) -&gt; None:
        if re.search(r&quot;\b[A-Z]\b&quot;, letter, re.IGNORECASE) and type(letter) == str:
            self.possibilities = [word for word in self.possibilities if not letter.upper() in word]
        else:
            raise TypeError(&quot;'letter' argument can only be one of the 26 letters of the alphabet.&quot;)

    @property
    def NumofPossibilities(self) -&gt; int:
        return len(self.possibilities)

...
possibilityNumber = inWord.NumofPossibilities  # you can still use it like this
</code></pre>

Q: Initialize multiple static data members via a function A: <p>I'd combine the best features of C++17 to achieve this:</p>
<ol>
<li><p><code>inline</code> objects for initialization order correction.</p>
</li>
<li><p><strong>Structured binding</strong> to produce proper names.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>The snippet would look like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>class MyFileSystem{
       std::tuple&lt;std::wstring, int, std::wstring, int&gt; init_class();
       static inline auto
       [ regularFolderType
       , regularIconIdx
       , softLinkType
       , softLinkIconIdx ]
       = init_class();
       // ...
}; //class MyFileSystem;
</code></pre>
<p>Now if <code>init_class</code> is to be inlined, I would go for an <strong>IILE</strong>(IIFE):</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>static inline auto
[/*variable names*/] =
[]{
     /*Do the prep*/
     return std::tuple
     {/*provide the values*/};
}(); // static variables def.
</code></pre>
<p>This is the simplest, and safest you can get it done in C++.</p>

Q: Why does std::vector sometimes reallocate even when I know how many elements I’ll add? A: <blockquote>
<p>How does reserve() affect reallocations and object construction?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>A <code>vector</code> has two separate things: a capacity and a size. The size is the number of objects that are currently in the vector. The capacity is the number of objects it <em>can</em> hold before it reallocates.</p>
<p><code>reserve()</code> assures that the capacity is (at least) as large as the number you pass, but it doesn't affect the current size (so it doesn't create any objects).</p>
<blockquote>
<p>When should I use reserve() instead of creating the vector with an initial size?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>You use <code>reserve()</code> when you want to allocate memory without creating any objects in that memory (yet). Creating with an initial size creates a vector that contains the specified number of objects, not just memory where you'll (later) be able to create objects. If you just specify the size, those objects will all be value initialized. If you want them all to have a specific value instead, you can pass that value and they'll all be constructed as copies of what you pass.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;vector&gt;
#include &lt;iostream&gt;

template &lt;class T&gt;
void print(char const *name, std::vector&lt;T&gt; const &amp;vals) {
    std::cout &lt;&lt; name;
    for (auto i : vals) { 
        std::cout &lt;&lt; i &lt;&lt; &quot; &quot;;
    }
    std::cout &lt;&lt; &quot;\n&quot;;
}

int main() {
    std::vector&lt;int&gt; a(3);
    std::vector&lt;int&gt; b(3, 5);

    print(&quot;a: &quot;, a);
    print(&quot;b: &quot;, b);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Result:</p>
<pre><code>a: 0 0 0 
b: 5 5 5 
</code></pre>
<blockquote>
<p>Is the vector’s growth strategy defined by the C++ standard, or does it depend on the implementation?</p>
</blockquote>
<p>It's partly prescribed, but partly depends on the implementation.</p>
<p>When it does reallocation, it needs to do a calculation like <code>capacity *= constant;</code>. It's <em>not</em> allowed to do something like <code>capacity += constant;</code>.</p>
<p>But the value of <code>constant</code> is not specified. Common values are 1.5 and 2.</p>
<p>But those are just the common values. In theory, somebody <em>could</em> use 10 or 100 or even 1000.</p>
<p>You do need to do an initial allocation and constant large enough to assure that when you do a reallocation, at least one new element is always added. If you used an initial size of 5 and a constant of 1.00001, when you tried to reallocate, the size wouldn't change, so you'd potentially go into an infinite loop of attempting reallocations, none of which actually increased the size at all.</p>
